Alembic migrations for PLIXOR.

The database URL is read from application settings (core.config.settings),
not from alembic.ini, so migrations and the app can never disagree about
which database they mean. Set DATABASE_URL in the environment before
running any command here.

Everyday use
------------

    alembic upgrade head            # apply everything outstanding
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"
    alembic downgrade -1            # step back one revision
    alembic current                 # what the database is at

Railway applies `alembic upgrade head` automatically via the Procfile's
release command.

Databases created before migrations existed
-------------------------------------------

The app used to build its schema by calling `Base.metadata.create_all()` on
startup. A database built that way holds all the tables but has no recorded
revision, so a plain `alembic upgrade head` tries to CREATE TABLE over tables
that exist and fails with "table users already exists".

You do not need to work out which case you are in:

    python -m scripts.bootstrap_migrations

It inspects the database and does the right thing -- stamps the baseline then
upgrades if the schema predates migrations, otherwise just upgrades. It is
idempotent, and `--dry-run` reports without changing anything. The Procfile
release command runs it, so deploys handle this on their own.

The decision is keyed on the recorded revision rather than on whether the
`alembic_version` table exists, because a failed upgrade leaves that table
behind empty; treating it as "already managed" would retry the same failure.

Doing it by hand instead, once per such database:

    alembic stamp head && alembic upgrade head
